Nice to have. I'll add attributes.\n\nAlso MPE per class then aligns with models. Fine, keep MPE as config_level by accuracy_class.\n\nrecommendation.accuracy_classes: [1, 2].\n\nNow write matrix_tables entry:\n{\n  \"name\": \"Flow characteristics by accuracy class variant\",\n  \"columns\": [\"accuracy_class\", \"q1\", \"q2\", \"q3\", \"q4\", \"q3_over_q1\", \"q2_over_q1\", \"q4_over_q3\"],\n  \"column_units\": [null, \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", null, null, null],\n  \"rows\": [\n    {\"accuracy_class\": 1, \"q1\": 6.4, \"q2\": 10.2, \"q3\": 1600, \"q4\": 2000, \"q3_over_q1\": 250, \"q2_over_q1\": 1.6, \"q4_over_q3\": 1.25},\n    {\"accuracy_class\": 2, \"q1\": 4.0, \"q2\": 6.4, \"q3\": 1600, \"q4\": 2000, \"q3_over_q1\": 400, \"q2_over_q1\": 1.6, \"q4_over_q3\": 1.25}\n  ],\n  \"description\": \"Type details for model MS2500 / MV110, nominal diameter 350 (DN350); two variants by accuracy class.\",\n  \"footnotes\": []\n}\n\nModel ids: I'll use \"DN350 class 1 (Q3/Q1=250)\" and \"DN350 class 2 (Q3/Q1=400)\". Hmm, shorter: \"class 1 / Q3Q1=250\". Slash in id might be awkward but fine. Let me use:\n- model_id: \"class1-Q3/Q1=250\"\n- model_id: \"class2-Q3/Q1=400\"\n\nHmm, ugly. Alternative: \"MS2500/MV110 class 1\" and \"MS2500/MV110 class 2\". Since DN350 is the only diameter in this fragment and it's type-level. I'll go:\n- {\"model_id\": \"class 1 (Q3/Q1=250)\", \"label\": \"MS2500 / MV110 DN350, accuracy class 1\", \"attributes\": {\"accuracy_class\": 1, \"q3_over_q1\": 250}}\n- {\"model_id\": \"class 2 (Q3/Q1=400)\", \"label\": \"MS2500 / MV110 DN350, accuracy class 2\", \"attributes\": {\"accuracy_class\": 2, \"q3_over_q1\": 400}}\n\nType-level characteristics list:\n- nominal_diameter: {value: 350, unit_symbol: null} (DN designation; unit could be \"DN\"? I'll put unit_symbol \"DN\" maybe... DN350 means nominal size 350 mm. unit_symbol null is safest; but hmm the caller looks up unit_symbol in unitsdb; null fine.)\n- measuring_principle: \"Electro