nless certain). I'll leave null or omit. Schema shows unit_id in shape; I'll include null.\n\nActually the value shape: {value, unit_symbol, unit_id, footnote_markers}. For string values like \"MAP16\", unit null. Should I include unit_id: null and footnote_markers: []? Keep it light: include value and unit_symbol; omit unit_id/footnote_markers unless relevant. The schema example shows those keys; but rule 8 says omit absent fields. I'll include unit_symbol (null when dimensionless string values). For compactness, for string values I'll still include unit_symbol: null. Footnote_markers: none exist in fragment; omit.\n\ncertificate object:\n{\n  \"number\": \"R49/2013-A-CZ1-2022.02\",\n  \"scheme\": \"A\",\n  \"page_total\": 16,\n  \"member_state\": \"CZ\"\n}\nproject_number? Not present. date_issued? Not present. oiml_issuer_id? Not present (CZ1 is in number but as issuer code... skip).\n\nissuing_authority: stamp mentions Český metrologický institut — partly visible stamp. It's an image annotation. I'd rather omit issuing_authority since it's not textual content... but the annotation clearly names it. \"Don't fabricate\" — the name IS present in the fragment (stamp text). I'll include minimal: {\"name\": \"Český metrologický institut\"}. Hmm. Risky? It's directly stated. I'll include it with just name. Actually, the instruction says omit sections not present; the stamp is present. I'll include name only.\n\nmanufacturers: Hemina SPA, Via Piemonte 2, Montagnana, 350 44 Italy. Address lines: [\"Via Piemonte 2\", \"Montagnana\", \"350 44 Italy\"]. Note \"350 44\" is the postal code (Montagnana PD). Keep as is.\n\napplicants: not present → omit.\n\ncertified_type:\n{\n  \"category\": \"Water meter\",\n  \"type_designations\": [\"MS2500 / MV110\"],\n  \"module_designation\": null? — R49 category: the meter is \"electromagnetic water meter\" compact/separate. module_designation maybe \"Not applicable\". I'll set null... or \"Not applicable\". Since R49 water meters full systems, module designation not applicable. I'll put \"Not applicable\". Hmm, don't fabricate — safer null. But category \"Water meter\" — is that the right category string? The examples list \"Gas meter\", \"Measuring system\"... water meter fits as \"Water meter\". Good.\n  \"description\": \"Electromagnetic water meter, nominal diameter 350, compact or separate version\" — description from measuring principle. I'll write \"Electromagnetic water meter (DN350)\". Keep simple: \"Electromagnetic water meter\".\n}\n\ncomponents: converter MV110, sensor. Also \"mini USB-B\" port. Components:\n[\n  {\"role\": \"converter (electronic unit, compact or separate version)\", \"type_designations\": [\"MV110\"], \"characteristics\": {}},\n  {\"role\": \"measuring sensor (primary element)\", \"type_designations\": [\"MS2500\"]}\n]\n\nHmm — is MS2500 the sensor or the complete meter? Text: \"The MS2500 / MV110 meters have to be sealed...\", \"Model number: MS2500 / MV110\". Could be sensor model + converter model. \"The compact water meter MS2500 / MV110 have to be sealed ... connecting the sensor\" — sensor separate from convector. I think MS2500 = sensor family, MV110 = converter. Reasonable. Alternatively skip components to avoid fabrication. I'll include but with cautious roles: \"sensor\" and \"converter\". The sealing text supports both terms. I'll include.\n\nNow let me double check the \"Indicating range 9 999 999\" — value 9999999 m³. Resolution 0.01 m³.\n\nSoftware version V.1.04.0005.0004 — string value. CRC \"9BA34F3B\".\n\nCut-off 0,2% → 0.2 %.\n\nNow assemble final JSON. Watch: no LaTeX, snake_case labels.\n\nmodel_level entries:\n\n[\n {\"attribute\": \"accuracy_class\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"values\": [{\"model\": \"class 1 (Q3/Q1=250)\", \"value\": 1}, {\"model\": \"class 2 (Q3/Q1=400)\", \"value\": 2}]},\n {\"attribute\": \"q1\", \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\", \"values\": [{\"model\": \"class 1 (Q3/Q1=250)\", \"value\": 6.4}, {\"model\": \"class 2 (Q3/Q1=400)\", \"value\": 4.0}]},\n {\"attribute\": \"q2\", ... 10.2 / 6.4},\n {\"attribute\": \"q3\", ... 1600 / 1600},\n {\"attribute\": \"q4\", ... 2000 / 2000},\n {\"attribute\": \"q3_over_q1\", ... 250 / 400}\n]\n\nSince q3, q4 same for both, could be type_level; but table structure puts them per-column; keeping model_level preserves the table. Either fine. I'll keep q3/q4 model_level to preserve matrix fidelity (same as R137 example where pmax constant 50 was only in matrix_tables, and model_level had only varying ones). Hmm, in the R137 example, model_level included only varying attributes (qmax, qt, qmin), constants stayed in matrix_tables. Follow that: model_level for q1, q2, q3_over_q1, accuracy_class; q3, q4 (constant) → type_level? q3=1600 both, q4=2000 both → type_level: q3 1600 m³/h, q4 2000 m³/h. And q2_over_q1, q4_over_q3 type_level. OK.\n\nBut then type_level has q3 and q4 single values — fine.\n\ntype_level:\n- q3: 1600 m³/h\n- q4: 2000 m³/h\n- q2_over_q1: 1.6\n- q4_over_q3: 1.25\n\nEverything else as listed.\n\nmatrix_tables rows: include full rows anyway (q3, q4 repeated) for fidelity.\n\nConfig values shape: {condition, value, unit_symbol}. Schema rule 5: values follow same structured form: {condition, value, unit_symbol}. Good.\n\nTemperature class values are strings like \"T90, T50, T30\" — value string.\n\nLiner conditions: \"PTFE liner\", \"Rilsan liner (from DN50)\", \"Ebonite liner (from DN200)\", \"Polypropylene liner\". The \"(from DN50)\" notes apply for availability across the family; include as part of condition.\n\nMaximum admissible temperature: conditions \"PTFE liner\": 90 °C; \"Polypropylene, Rilsan and Ebonite liner\": 50 °C.\n\nPower supply axis conditions: \"MS2500 / MV110 HV\", \"MS2500 / MV110 LV\", \"MS2500 / MV110 LLV\".\n\nInternal backup battery voltage: 3.7 V (converted from 3,7). Capacity 5200 mAh. Type \"Battery\".\n\nNow also include \"resolution_of_device_for_rapid_testing\" — value \"-\", missing → omit (rule 8). Good. \"Mounting: -\" omit. \"Wiring instructions: -\" omit. \"Minimum battery life time: -\" omit.\n\nFinal JSON structure order: certificate, issuing_authority, manufacturers, certified_type, model_family, components, characteristics, matrix_tables, recommendation.\n\nWrite it out.\n\nAlso check \"Environmental class: O (-25 to +55 °C)\" — value \"O\", plus separate env temp range.\n\nPage total 16 — include. scheme \"A\" at certificate level.\n\nOne more: \"Designation of the module for all nominal diameter\" — implies modules HV/LV/LLV apply across all nominal diameters. Could note in matrix description.\n\nLet me write the JSON now carefully, valid JSON, no trailing commas.",
